 - **Performance:** Stable for ill-conditioned systems [S1, unofficial]. Generally slower than direct solvers for moderately sized problems but preferred for numerical stability in large or rank-deficient cases [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Robust to noise (especially with Adam), handles ill-conditioned problems, memory efficient (matrix-free).
-- **Limitations:** Per-parameter filtering can cause high-frequency artifacts in noisy or anisotropic settings [S3]. Convergence can be slow compared to second-order methods for well-conditioned problems.
-- **When to use:** Large-scale inverse volume rendering, ill-conditioned systems, when memory is constrained.
-- **When not to use:** When high-frequency artifacts are unacceptable and second-order methods are feasible, or when exact line search is required for rapid convergence.
